<div class="container">
<h1 class="doc-h1">Session workday standard</h1>
<p class="doc-muted">Last updated Fri Nov 06 2026. Version 0.1. Status: in use on main.</p>

<div id="toc" class="row">
<div class="col-100">
<p class="doc-h3">Contents</p>
<p class="doc-navrow"><a class="doc-navlink" href="#why">Why</a></p>
<p class="doc-navrow"><a class="doc-navlink" href="#prompt">First prompt</a></p>
<p class="doc-navrow"><a class="doc-navlink" href="#check">Interval check</a></p>
<p class="doc-navrow"><a class="doc-navlink" href="#freshness">Stale docs, projects and todos</a></p>
<p class="doc-navrow"><a class="doc-navlink" href="#day">End of day</a></p>
</div>
</div>

<h2 id="why" class="doc-h2">Why</h2>
<p class="doc-body">A Hermes dashboard left up overnight keeps the host lock. The next morning's open binds a port and is observe-only. A todo set to status 5 with no log row has no time. An open log left for days bills nothing honest until it is closed. The check names those. It does not invent hours.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">Back to top</a></p>

<h2 id="prompt" class="doc-h2">First prompt</h2>
<p class="doc-body">On the first prompt of a session:</p>
<ol class="doc-body">
<li>Say what the prompt is about.</li>
<li>Read sitename, username, and roles. <code>GET /api/workday/check</code> returns them when the caller is logged in. A LAN call has no user, so the role class is guest.</li>
<li>Guest and normal get an assist log: what the assistant did. Developer and admin get a work log: what was done on the todo.</li>
<li>Search for a todo about the question. Do not create a twin.</li>
<li>Start that todo with <code>POST /api/todo/open_log</code> before any other write. Prove it with <code>work_status</code>.</li>
<li>If the task is not finished, Stop with <code>close_log</code>. If it is finished, Done with <code>done_with_log</code>. Do not leave status 5.</li>
<li>Close the session: docs, the plan, and the todo log.</li>
</ol>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">Back to top</a></p>

<h2 id="check" class="doc-h2">Interval check</h2>
<p class="doc-body"><code>script/workday_standard.pl --check</code> is read-only. It reports open logs older than 12 hours, status-5 todos with no log row, Hermes processes, branch servers other than :4006, and branch HEADs older than 14 days that are still ahead of main. Exit 2 means something was found. The same report is <code>GET /api/workday/check</code>.</p>
<p class="doc-body">On this workstation the check runs every 30 minutes from the user crontab, against the main checkout. Other workstations get the script on the next merge. Add the same crontab line there. The health evaluator does not run from a PycharmProjects checkout, so cron is the interval.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">Back to top</a></p>

<h2 id="freshness" class="doc-h2">Stale docs, projects and todos</h2>
<p class="doc-body">The same report carries a <code>freshness</code> section: plans that no longer match their code or their project rows, projects that should be closed or revived, and todos whose status no longer matches their clock. <code>script/workday_standard.pl --freshness</code> prints that section alone (<code>GET /api/workday/check</code> returns all of it).</p>
<p class="doc-body">Rules and thresholds: a status-2 todo with no activity for <b>$STALE_TODO_D = 30</b> days is an abandoned start; a project with no todo activity for 30 days is dormant, and a project whose todos are all done or cancelled should be closed; a plan page is stale when its project's rows moved <b>14</b> days after the page last changed. Freshness is measured from git commit time per file, because most pages have no usable <code>last_updated</code> META field - a page whose META date is older than its own commit is reported separately as <code>fix_meta</code>. The backlog pass runs at 30 days, then those first two thresholds drop to 7.</p>
<p class="doc-body">Scope is the branch and its responsibilities. A feature branch declares one <code>project_id</code> in <code>worktrees.json</code> and is audited only against it: its own todos, its own pages. Rows outside that scope are counted as skipped, never listed. <code>main</code> is the exception and is declared <code>scope: "all"</code> - it is the branch we build from, the merge point for every branch, and the proving ground for the docker image that becomes the production version, so it is responsible for every project. A branch with neither a <code>project_id</code> nor <code>scope: "all"</code> runs unscoped and the report says so (<code>scope.declared = 0</code>).</p>
<p class="doc-body">Findings are filed to the branch that owns the work, not to the trunk. <code>route_findings</code> reads each finding's <code>project_id</code>, looks up the branch that declares that project, and files one todo per owning branch per group - so the 3D branch gets its own stale todos, git gets its own, schema gets its own. main keeps only what no branch owns: undocumented code surfaces, and projects no branch has claimed. Those go to <code>project_id 311</code> (Release Engineering), which owns the gate, the version cut and the image. Two branches declaring one project id is reported as <code>drift</code> in the same payload, because it means two owners for one backlog (schema once said 138, planning's id; schema owns 5 "Schema managment").</p>
<p class="doc-body">Todo subjects carry no counts - <code>Stale todos on git</code>, not <code>... on git: 23 need a decision</code> - because a count that moves every 30 minutes would file a new todo on every run. The count and the evidence go in the description, dated, so the same todo is updated in place.</p>
<p class="doc-body">Status 5 is repaired by evidence, not by guessing: unfinished work uses <code>close_log</code> (row returns to 2), finished work uses <code>done_with_log</code>. <code>close_log</code> accepts <code>duration_mins</code>, and that is where real or estimated time from the branch's session goes. The only field corrected is the log end time; a span over 1440 minutes is capped as a day, with the correction written into the log summary.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><code>script/workday_standard.pl --raise-audit-todos</code> files the findings as at most one todo per owning branch per group - stale todos, stale projects, stale documentation. A second run updates that open todo instead of creating a twin, so the 30-minute cron cannot fill the list. The audit's own todos are excluded from the stale set.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#toc">Back to top</a></p>

<h2 id="day" class="doc-h2">End of day</h2>
<p class="doc-body"><code>script/workday_standard.pl --end-of-day</code> and <code>POST /api/workday/end_of_day</code> with <code>confirm:1</code> stop every registered branch server except :4006 and signal Hermes dashboard, gateway, and TUI processes. :4006 stays. Todo logs are not closed by that action, because closing a 166-hour clock would bill time that was not work. Close those with <code>close_log</code> and say they were left open.</p>
<p class="doc-body">The 22:00 crontab line on this host runs that stop. Change the hour in crontab if the day ends earlier.</p>
<p class="doc-footer">Files: Comserv/lib/Comserv/Util/WorkdayStandard.pm, Comserv/script/workday_standard.pl, Comserv/lib/Comserv/Controller/Api.pm.</p>
</div>
